lot"><span class="time">118–120</span><div><strong>Closure + homework</strong><br/>Revise the paragraph and bring it to Week 2.</div></div></div></section><section><div class="head"><div><span class="label">0–18 minutes</span><h2>Welcome &amp; Course Orientation</h2></div><p>Begin by identifying the Academic English areas that matter most for your MSc study.</p></div><div class="grid"><div class="card"><h3>Discussion Prompts</h3><ol><li>Which is harder for you: <strong>reading, vocabulary, grammar, writing, or speaking</strong>?</li><li>What makes a sentence sound <strong>academic</strong>?</li><li>Is your writing usually too <strong>simple, long, vague, or grammatically inaccurate</strong>?</li><li>When describing engineering work, do you use <strong>exact measurements</strong> or general descriptions?</li></ol><div class="takeaway"><strong>Key point:</strong> this course develops your ability to <strong>communicate engineering knowledge in English</strong>.</div></div><div class="card"><h3>Course Facts</h3><ul><li><strong>15 weeks</strong> · 120 minutes per normal week.</li><li><strong>Week 8:</strong> Midterm examination.</li><li><strong>Week 15:</strong> Review only.</li><li><strong>Assessment:</strong> Homework (5) + Quiz 1 (5) + Midterm (15) + Quiz 2 (5) = <strong>30</strong> · Final = <strong>70</strong>.</li><li>Final examination = <strong>70 marks</strong>.</li></ul></div></div></section><section id="academic"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">18–33 minutes</span><h2>What Makes English Academic?</h2></div><p>Academic language is selected for a specific <strong>audience, purpose and discipline</strong>.</p></div><div class="grid4"><div class="card"><h3>Formal</h3><p>Avoid <strong class="key">casual or conversational expressions</strong> such as <em>stuff</em>, <em>a lot of</em>, and <em>really big</em> when more precise alternatives are available.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Objective</h3><p>Report <strong class="key">evidence, measurements and observations</strong> without unnecessary exaggeration or emotional judgement.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Precise</h3><p>Use <strong class="key">numbers, quantities, units, technical terms and specific verbs</strong> rather than general impressions.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Clear</h3><p>Use <strong class="key">controlled sentence structure</strong> so the reader can understand the intended meaning efficiently.</p></div></div><div class="professional-rule"></div><div class="grid"><div class="card definition"><h3>Academic ≠ Complicated</h3><p>Do not choose a long or unusual word merely to sound academic. Choose the word that expresses the meaning <strong class="key">most accurately</strong>.</p><div class="good"><strong>Better principle:</strong><strong>clear + precise + appropriate</strong> is stronger than “complex for the sake of complexity.”</div></div><div class="card definition"><h3>Objectivity ≠ No First Person Ever</h3><p>Different disciplines have different conventions. The practical rule for this course is to avoid <strong class="key">unnecessary personal or emotional wording</strong> and to keep the focus on <strong class="key">evidence and technical meaning</strong>.</p></div></div><div class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><strong>Roadmap:</strong> Week 2 develops <strong>academic vocabulary</strong>, Week 3 <strong>tense control</strong>, Week 4 <strong>descriptive engineering language</strong>, Week 7 <strong>sentence structure</strong>, and Week 9 <strong>cohesion and coherence</strong>.</div></section><section id="register"><div class="head"><div><span class="label">33–60 minutes</span><h2>Formal Register: From Conversation to Academic English</h2></div><p>Students identify exactly <strong>what changed</strong> between the informal and academic versions.</p></div><table><tr><th>Less academic / vague</th><th>More academic / controlled</th><th>What changed?</th></tr><tr><td>We <strong>looked at</strong> the problem.</td><td>The fault was <strong>investigated using time-domain simulation</strong> in MATLAB/Simulink.</td><td>Vague verb → <strong>specific method</strong>.<br/><span class="small-note">The passive voice is a <strong>choice</strong> here, not a rule.</span></td></tr><tr><td>The results were <strong>good</strong>.</td><td>The proposed controller reduced <strong>total harmonic distortion from 8.4% to 2.1%</strong>.</td><td>Evaluation → <strong>measured evidence</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>The system is <strong>small</strong>.</td><td>The prototype occupies <strong>10 cm² of PCB area</strong>, <strong>65% less</strong> than the previous design.</td><td>Adjective → <strong>dimension + comparison</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>The signal changed <strong>quickly</strong>.</td><td>The output voltage <strong>rose from 3.0 V to 5.0 V within 10 s</strong>, then settled at <strong>4.8 V (±2%)</strong>.</td><td>Impression → <strong>values + trend + tolerance</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>The motor <strong>got hot</strong>.</td><td>The <strong>stator winding temperature reached 78 °C</strong> after <strong>30 min at rated load</strong>.</td><td>Informal verb → <strong>quantity + condition</strong>.</td></tr></table><div class="takeaway" style="margin-top:12px"><span class="tag">Key point</span><strong>Academic ≠ passive.</strong> The first example uses the passive because the method is more important than the researcher. First person can still be appropriate when disciplinary conventions allow it.</div><div class="grid" style="margin-top:14px"><div class="task"><h3>Activity A — Spot the Problem</h3><p>Identify the main issue: <strong>informal, personal, vague, emotive, or imprecise</strong>.</p><div class="prompt">1. The circuit worked <strong>really well</strong>.</div><input placeholder="Problem + improved wording" type="text"/><div class="prompt">2. We got <strong>a lot of noise</strong> in the signal.</div><input placeholder="Problem + improved wording" type="text"/><div class="prompt">3. The new design is <strong>amazing</strong>.</div><input placeholder="Problem + improved wording" type="text"/><div class="student-answer-note">Answer key: reviewed in class.</div></div><div class="task"><h3>Activity B — Nominalisation Preview</h3><div class="bad">The device <strong>failed to operate because the voltage dropped</strong>.</div><div class="example-pair"><div></div><div class="arrow">↓</div><div></div></div><div class="good"><strong>Device failure</strong> resulted from a <strong>voltage drop</strong>.</d